   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to prohibiting student loan payment history checks in making employment decisions",
    "basePrintNo": "S4483",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4483-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §219-d, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4483\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the labor law, in relation to prohibiting student loan\npayment history checks in making employment decisions\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill protects individual privacy by providing that an employer's\nrequest for student loan payment history for the purposes of\nemployment is an unlawful discriminatory practice\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the labor law by adding a new section 219-d to\nprovide that except in cases mandated by law, it shall be an unlawful\ndiscriminatory practice for an employer to request or use for\nemployment purposes information contained in the consumer credit\nhistory the payment status of a student loan of an applicant for\nemployment or otherwise discriminate against such applicant with\nregard to hiring decisions.\n\nSection two defines consumer credit history to mean any information\nbearing on an individual's credit worthiness, credit standing, or\ncredit capacity, including but not limited to an individual's credit\nscore, credit account and other consumer account balances, and payment\nhistory.\n\nSection three sets an immediate effective date\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAs tuition costs continue to rise, student loans play an integral role\nin a student's ability to fund their college and graduate education.\nWithout such mechanisms, students would be unable to obtain an\neducation to succeed in a competitive job market. The fact that\nstudents take advantage of student loans should not be used against\nthem. This bill will protect a prospective employee's right to privacy\nas to the status on their student loan payments.  Specifically, this\nbill provides that an employer's request for the payment status of an\napplicant's student loan payment is an unlawful discriminatory\npractice. By prohibiting such practice, applicants will be rewarded\nbased on the education they have received, not the cost they bore to\nobtain it.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
