    "lawCode": "Add §219-d, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4483A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn  act  to amend the labor law, in relation to prohibiting student loan\npayment history checks in making employment decisions\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill protects individual privacy by providing  that  an  employer's\nrequest  for student loan payment history for the purposes of employment\nis an unlawful discriminatory practice\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the labor law  by  adding  a  new  section  219-d  to\nprovide  that  except  in cases mandated by law, it shall be an unlawful\ndiscriminatory practice for an employer to request or use for employment\npurposes information  contained  in  the  consumer  credit  history  the\npayment  status  of  a  student  loan  of an applicant for employment or\notherwise discriminate against such  applicant  with  regard  to  hiring\ndecisions.\n\nSection  two  defines  consumer  credit  history to mean any information\nbearing on an individual's credit worthiness, credit standing, or credit\ncapacity, including but not limited to  an  individual's  credit  score,\ncredit account and other consumer account balances, and payment history.\n\nSection  three  provides an exception for employers required by state or\nfederal law or regulations or  by  a  self-regulatory  organization,  as\ndefined  in  paragraph twenty-six of subdivision (a) of section three of\nthe securities exchange act of 1934.\n\nSection four sets an immediate effective date\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs tuition costs continue to rise, student loans play an  integral  role\nin  a  student's  ability  to fund their college and graduate education.\nWithout such mechanisms, students would be unable to obtain an education\nto succeed in a competitive job market.  The  fact  that  students  take\nadvantage  of  student  loans should not be used against them. This bill\nwill protect a prospective employee's right to privacy as to the  status\non  their  student loan payments.  Specifically, this bill provides that\nan employer's request for the payment status of an  applicant's  student\nloan payment is an unlawful discriminatory practice. By prohibiting such\npractice,  applicants  will be rewarded based on the education they have\nreceived, not the cost they bore to obtain it.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
